INSERT INTO "EntityTranslation" (id, "entityId", locale, title, "shortDescription", essay, excerpt, "createdAt", "updatedAt")
VALUES
('batch001-en-picasso','cmsvvxi9700hc85sjjoidcq8a','en','Pablo Picasso',
 'Pablo Picasso (Málaga, 1881–Mougins, 1973) was an academically trained artist who developed a decisive language of modern art in Paris. His career moved from a break with traditional representation to the political force of the 1937 commission for the Spanish Pavilion.',
 'Pablo Picasso was born in Málaga in 1881, trained in the academic tradition of fine arts, and lived in Barcelona at the turn of the century. He settled in Paris in 1904, where his work became known for breaking with inherited systems of representation.\n\nHis practice also revisited classical mythology and works from the artistic tradition, while drawing on non-naturalistic sources such as African masks. In 1937 he was commissioned to make a large painting for the Spanish Pavilion at the International Exposition in Paris; that context led to Guernica, one of the central works of his career.',
 'Pablo Picasso (Málaga, 1881–Mougins, 1973) was an academically trained artist who developed a decisive language of modern art in Paris.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-cubismo','cmsvvxhgm002r85sjdntcbn33','en','Cubismo',
 'Cubism was one of the most influential styles of the twentieth century. Emerging around 1907 through a new approach to visual representation, it opened possibilities that fed several later abstract languages.',
 'Cubism is generally considered to have begun around 1907, when Les Demoiselles d’Avignon already contained cubist elements. The name seems to derive from a comment by Louis Vauxcelles about Georges Braque’s paintings exhibited in Paris in 1908, which the critic described as reducing forms to geometric outlines and cubes.\n\nCubism was not simply an isolated style. It transformed the treatment of visual reality and became a starting point for later abstract styles, including Constructivism and Neoplasticism, as Tate documents.',
 'Cubism was one of the most influential styles of the twentieth century.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-venus','cmsvvxif800ku85sjmrawpu0e','en','El nacimiento de Venus',
 'Painted by Sandro Botticelli around 1484–1486, The Birth of Venus shows the goddess of love and beauty arriving on Cyprus, carried by the wind. Its ambitious use of tempera on canvas belongs to the material and social world of fifteenth-century Florentine painting.',
 'The Birth of Venus was painted around 1484–1486 in tempera on canvas and measures 172.5 × 278.5 cm. It is held by the Uffizi Gallery in Florence. Although it is known as The Birth of Venus, the composition shows Venus arriving on the island of Cyprus, born from sea foam and carried by Zephyr and perhaps Aura.\n\nThe support matters. Canvas was widely used during the fifteenth century for decorative works intended for noble households. The painting can therefore be understood through both its mythological image and the material practices of its time.',
 'Painted by Sandro Botticelli around 1484–1486, The Birth of Venus shows the goddess of love and beauty arriving on Cyprus, carried by the wind.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-maman','cmqmnymtz004o4vsj0wtos3ox','en','Maman',
 'Maman is a monumental sculpture by Louise Bourgeois that brings motherhood, care, and threat together in an architectural spider. Bronze, stainless steel, and marble turn the sculptural body into an experience of scale and space.',
 'Made in 1999, Maman measures approximately 927 × 891 × 1024 cm and is linked to the Guggenheim Bilbao collection. It uses bronze, stainless steel, and marble, materials that make the tension between weight, fragility, and protection visible.\n\nThe spider occupies the surrounding space and places the viewer beneath it. Its published connections with motherhood, care, monument, assemblage, and materiality show that its scale is not decorative; it is part of the work’s sculptural meaning.',
 'Maman is a monumental sculpture by Louise Bourgeois that brings motherhood, care, and threat together in an architectural spider.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-meninas','cmqmnymu5004r4vsjrvya8fdp','en','Las Meninas',
 'Painted by Diego Velázquez in 1656, Las Meninas is an oil painting on canvas that organizes a court scene around looking, representation, and the viewer’s position. It is held by the Museo del Prado and measures 318 × 276 cm.',
 'Velázquez painted Las Meninas in 1656 in oil on canvas. The work is held by the Museo del Prado in Madrid and is connected with the Baroque, portraiture, the gaze, representation, and linear perspective.\n\nIts interest lies not only in who appears in the scene, but in how the painting distributes the positions of those who look, are looked at, and seem to participate in making the image. The courtly room becomes a visual problem: the painting shows a scene while also making the act of representation visible.',
 'Painted by Diego Velázquez in 1656, Las Meninas is an oil painting on canvas that organizes a court scene around looking, representation, and the viewer’s position.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-saturn','cmqmnymt100494vsjefqf57vp','en','Saturno devorando a su hijo',
 'Saturn Devouring His Son belongs to Francisco de Goya’s Black Paintings, made between 1820 and 1823. Its deformed body, violence, and darkness turn myth into an extreme image of time, destruction, and fear.',
 'The work was made between 1820 and 1823 in oil transferred to canvas and measures 143.5 × 81.4 cm. It is held by the Museo del Prado in Madrid as part of the Black Paintings.\n\nThe god devouring his child is not treated as a distant narrative. Scale, paint, and Saturn’s expression concentrate violence in the body and make it an immediate visual experience. This is why the work connects so strongly with violence, time, and pain.',
 'Saturn Devouring His Son belongs to Francisco de Goya’s Black Paintings, made between 1820 and 1823.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-duchamp','cmqmnymsu00474vsjen0o2yni','en','Marcel Duchamp',
 'Marcel Duchamp (1887–1968) was a French-American artist who shifted attention from making an object to choosing it, framing it, and asking what can count as art. His practice crosses painting, objects, and conceptual experimentation.',
 'Duchamp was born in Blainville-Crevon in 1887 and died in 1968. He is situated between France and the United States and is identified with objects, painting, and conceptual practice.\n\nHis importance lies in the way authorship, choice, play, and the definition of art become part of the work. Fountain and Bottle Rack make it possible to follow how a decision and a change of context can become the centre of an artistic experience.',
 'Marcel Duchamp (1887–1968) was a French-American artist who shifted attention from making an object to choosing it, framing it, and asking what can count as art.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-nighthawks','cmqmnymui004v4vsjjq7adr8c','en','Nighthawks',
 'Nighthawks, painted by Edward Hopper in 1942, brings together a night scene, artificial light, and figures isolated inside an urban setting. The oil painting on canvas is held by the Art Institute of Chicago and measures 84.1 × 152.4 cm.',
 'Edward Hopper painted Nighthawks in 1942 in oil on canvas. The work is held by the Art Institute of Chicago and is connected with the city and with realism.\n\nThe composition focuses attention on a lit interior separated from the street by glass. Architecture, the distance between the figures, and the absence of a closed narrative make waiting and isolation part of the image as much as its characters.',
 'Nighthawks, painted by Edward Hopper in 1942, brings together a night scene, artificial light, and figures isolated inside an urban setting.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-hopper','cmqmnymsm00454vsjw8djfbo0','en','Edward Hopper',
 'Edward Hopper (1882–1967) was an American painter who turned urban scenes, architecture, light, and moments of waiting into lasting images of modern life. His work is associated with the city, solitude, and everyday experience.',
 'Hopper was born in Nyack in 1882 and died in 1967. He was an American painter and printmaker.\n\nHis relationship with the city and with works such as Nighthawks reveals a persistent concern with everyday spaces and with the distance that can appear even when people share the same place. Light and architecture are not neutral backgrounds; they organize the characters’ experience.',
 'Edward Hopper (1882–1967) was an American painter who turned urban scenes, architecture, light, and moments of waiting into lasting images of modern life.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-bacon','cmqmnymsr00464vsj35ef244h','en','Francis Bacon',
 'Francis Bacon (1909–1992) developed a painting centred on bodily vulnerability, distortion, and psychological intensity. His work turns the human figure into a site of tension between presence, violence, and enclosure.',
 'Francis Bacon was born in Dublin in 1909 and died in 1992. He is situated between Ireland and the United Kingdom and is identified primarily with painting.\n\nIn his work, the body does not appear as a stable or idealized form. Distortion, flesh, and enclosed space produce an image subjected to difficult psychological forces. This tension explains the relationship between his work and subjects in which power, violence, fear, and representation meet.',
 'Francis Bacon (1909–1992) developed a painting centred on bodily vulnerability, distortion, and psychological intensity.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-fountain','cmqmnymuo004x4vsjni2c67mr','en','Fountain',
 'Fountain is a 1917 ready-made by Marcel Duchamp. By presenting a porcelain object as art, it shifts attention toward choice, authorship, and the institutional frame that decides what can be recognized as art.',
 'Fountain is dated 1917 and identified as a porcelain ready-made. The provenance relations record replicas in museum collections rather than a single surviving original object.\n\nIts importance lies in a change of frame. The gesture is not the modelling of a new form, but the selection of an object and its placement before the art system. The work therefore makes the relationship between object, authorship, and institutional definition concrete.',
 'Fountain is a 1917 ready-made by Marcel Duchamp. By presenting a porcelain object as art, it shifts attention toward choice, authorship, and the institutional frame that decides what can be recognized as art.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-bottle','cmqmnymuu004z4vsjje52odxv','en','Bottle Rack',
 'Bottle Rack is a metal ready-made by Marcel Duchamp, dated 1914. It turns a functional object into a question about choice, use, and artistic value.',
 'Bottle Rack is dated 1914 and recorded as a metal ready-made; the provenance information indicates that museum replicas exist. Its operation is simple but decisive: an object is separated from the function for which it was made and placed in an artistic context.\n\nThe work shows why Duchamp is central to discussions of authorship and the object. Its meaning does not come from visible craft alone, but from the decision that reorganizes the viewer’s attention.',
 'Bottle Rack is a metal ready-made by Marcel Duchamp, dated 1914. It turns a functional object into a question about choice, use, and artistic value.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-guernica','cmsvvxiio00mv85sj920czcuo','en','Guernica',
 'Guernica, painted by Pablo Picasso in 1937, is a large oil painting on canvas associated with the Spanish Civil War. Its scale and fractured figures turn violence and memory into a public visual experience.',
 'Guernica was painted in 1937 in oil on canvas and measures 349.3 × 776.6 cm. It is held by the Museo Nacional Centro de Arte Reina Sofía in Madrid. Its published relations connect it with Pablo Picasso, the Spanish Civil War, war, violence, exile, memory, propaganda, and history painting.\n\nThe work does not describe a scene of war through a linear narrative. Broken bodies, animals, cries, and signs make destruction collective. Its large format strengthens that public dimension: the painting is not only an episode, but a visual form of memory about violence.',
 'Guernica, painted by Pablo Picasso in 1937, is a large oil painting on canvas associated with the Spanish Civil War.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-baca','cmtptpmi90002gxss42zn1ydw','en','Judith Baca',
 'Judith Baca is a painter, muralist, and scholar who works collaboratively with communities to create sites of public memory. Her initiatives attend especially to historically marginalized populations and make the mural a social practice.',
 'Judith Baca works with communities to create sites of public memory. The testimony preserved by the Smithsonian American Art Museum relates this practice to women, the working poor, youth, older people, LGBTQ+ communities, and immigrant communities.\n\nHer work shows that a mural can be an image, a collective process, and a place where a community makes its own history visible. Its public dimension is not an addition to the work; it is part of its method and meaning.',
 'Judith Baca is a painter, muralist, and scholar who works collaboratively with communities to create sites of public memory.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-albers','cmsvvxj8a012s85sjedihcwi5','en','Josef Albers',
 'Josef Albers (1888–1976) was an artist and teacher whose work at Black Mountain College was shaped by practical learning and by principles he brought from the Bauhaus. His activity connected artistic experiment, pedagogy, and communal life.',
 'Josef Albers was at Black Mountain College from 1933 to 1949 and was hired as its first art teacher. The surviving sources describe a pedagogy based on practice before theory, learning by doing, and the idea that art belongs within a general education.\n\nThe connection with the Bauhaus matters because it shows how those principles moved into another context. At Black Mountain College, studying art did not mean isolating it from intellectual life; it meant questioning disciplinary hierarchies and understanding experience as part of learning.',
 'Josef Albers (1888–1976) was an artist and teacher whose work at Black Mountain College was shaped by practical learning and by principles he brought from the Bauhaus.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-bmc','cmsvvxiy500w485sjaswn4h4o','en','Black Mountain College',
 'Black Mountain College was an educational institution associated with artistic experiment, communal life, and the arrival of artists and intellectuals displaced by European fascism. Its practices combined study, collective work, and cultural activity.',
 'Sources from the Black Mountain College Museum + Arts Center describe a community in which teachers and students prepared costumes and decorations for themed parties, including celebrations connected with Dada and Ancient Greece. A digital exhibition also links the college’s history to the exile caused by the rise of fascism in Europe.\n\nThe institution matters as a meeting place between education, art, and communal life. Josef Albers’s presence and the reception of Bauhaus principles show how teaching could be organized around practice, collaboration, and experience.',
 'Black Mountain College was an educational institution associated with artistic experiment, communal life, and the arrival of artists and intellectuals displaced by European fascism.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-wave','cmsvvxile00og85sjyt9ud6rp','en','La gran ola de Kanagawa',
 'The Great Wave off Kanagawa is a colour woodblock print by Katsushika Hokusai, dated around 1831, made with ink and colour on paper. It is associated with ukiyo-e and turns landscape and the force of the sea into an image of formal tension.',
 'The work is dated around 1831 and measures 25.7 × 37.9 cm. It is recorded as a colour woodblock print made with ink and colour on paper and is held by The Metropolitan Museum of Art in New York. Its published relations connect it with Hokusai, ukiyo-e, landscape, nature, paper, and woodblock printing.\n\nThe wave organizes the image as a force that dominates the visual field. The work shows how a multiple-print technique can construct a scene of great precision, rhythm, and tension between nature and human presence.',
 'The Great Wave off Kanagawa is a colour woodblock print by Katsushika Hokusai, dated around 1831, made with ink and colour on paper.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-nefertiti','cmsvvxidz00k385sjl9wykswf','en','Busto de Nefertiti',
 'The bust of Nefertiti is a limestone and stucco sculpture, 47 cm high, connected in the graph with Egyptian art, portraiture, and tradition. Its interest lies in the concentration of identity, representation, and materiality within a human-scale image.',
 'The bust is made of limestone and stucco and is 47 cm high. Its published relations connect it with Egyptian art, portraiture, iconography, the body, pigment, gold, and tradition, as well as with the British Museum and the Egyptian Museum in Cairo.\n\nIts scale permits a close encounter with the represented face, while the materials remind us that the image is not appearance alone: it is also surface, colour, and sculptural labour. Portraiture appears here as a material construction of identity.',
 'The bust of Nefertiti is a limestone and stucco sculpture, 47 cm high, connected with Egyptian art, portraiture, and tradition.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-olympia','cmsvvxihi00m785sje6zpfn92','en','Olympia',
 'Olympia, painted by Édouard Manet in 1863, is an oil painting on canvas connected with the body, desire, the nude, the gaze, and gender. Its place within realism can be understood through the tension between the tradition of the nude and the direct presence of the figure.',
 'Olympia was painted in 1863 in oil on canvas and measures 130.5 × 190 cm. It is held by the Musée d’Orsay in Paris. Its published relations connect it with Édouard Manet, realism, the nude, the body, desire, sexuality, gender, and the gaze.\n\nThese connections place the work at a point of tension. It uses a recognizable format from art history, but makes the figure’s presence and gaze interrupt the idealized distance of the traditional nude. The viewer is drawn into a scene in which looking also means occupying a social position.',
 'Olympia, painted by Édouard Manet in 1863, is an oil painting on canvas connected with the body, desire, the nude, the gaze, and gender.',now(),now()),
('batch001-en-may','cmsvvxigt00ls85sj3rcd1lec','en','El 3 de mayo de 1808',
 'The Third of May 1808, painted by Francisco de Goya in 1814, is an oil painting on canvas that represents war and death through the vulnerability of those who suffer them. Held by the Museo del Prado, it measures 268 × 347 cm.',
 'Goya painted The Third of May 1808 in 1814 in oil on canvas. It measures 268 × 347 cm and is held by the Museo del Prado in Madrid. Its published relations connect it with war, death, violence, Romanticism, printmaking, and Francisco de Goya.\n\nThe light concentrates the scene on the victims and on the instant before execution. The painting does not turn violence into an ordered feat; it makes fear, exposed bodies, and the inequality between those who shoot and those who wait visible. Its historical force comes from that point of view.',
 'The Third of May 1808, painted by Francisco de Goya in 1814, is an oil painting on canvas that represents war and death through the vulnerability of those who suffer them.',now(),now())
